; pi4I2cScl.pi4 - WHAT CLOCK ACTUALLY FEEDS THE I2C DIVIDER? Measure it.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; THE QUESTION. The BCM2711 datasheet (Table 30, p.28) says SCL =
; core_clock / CDIV "where core_clk is nominally 150 MHz", and the driver
; computed its reported rate from that constant. Meanwhile the monitor's
; own `info` prints a MEASURED VideoCore clock of half a gigahertz on the
; same board in the same session. If the divider is fed from the second
; number rather than the first, every rate this board has ever reported is
; wrong by that ratio - a requested 100 kHz would really be about 333 kHz.
;
; That was an inference drawn from two printed numbers. Nothing had ever
; watched a clock edge. This watches clock edges.
;
; HOW. The divider is programmed to a KNOWN CDIV, a transaction is started
; (a one-byte read from an address with nothing on it - the address is not
; acknowledged, but the controller still clocks SCL out to ask), and the
; SCL pad is sampled in a tight loop while it happens. Every falling edge
; is timestamped from the architectural counter, so
;
;     SCL    = gaps * tick_hz / (t_last - t_first)
;     source = SCL * CDIV
;
; over a run of gaps that are all one clock period. The source clock then
; falls out of two things both measured on this board: an edge count and
; a tick count.
;
; WHICH GAPS COUNT IS THE WHOLE ACCURACY OF THIS THING, and getting it
; wrong cost a wrong answer that looked right - see THE FIRST GAP IS NOT
; A CLOCK PERIOD, further down, which is where the intervals are printed
; and the reason is set out with the numbers that showed it.
;
; REPEATED AT FIVE DIVIDERS PLUS WHATEVER WAS SET WHEN IT STARTED, so the
; answer is a fit and not a point. If the rows agree, the divider is fed
; by one clock and its rate is that number. If they walk with CDIV, the
; model SCL = source/CDIV is wrong and this prints disagreeing numbers
; instead of one confident one.
;
; THE SAMPLING RATE IS PRINTED AND SO IS THE MARGIN. A polling loop cannot
; see an edge it did not sample near, so every row reports how many samples
; fell in one SCL half-period AND how far the worst fitted gap was from
; their mean. Below four samples a half the row says it is not evidence
; rather than being quietly averaged in with the good ones.
;
; A SECOND ESTIMATE runs alongside: the same fitted span at two different
; dividers, subtracted. That removes the one error a single row cannot -
; the time the sampling loop takes to NOTICE an edge - because it is a
; property of the loop and not of the divider. See THE SLOPE.
;
; AND THE ANSWER IS COMPARED WITH WHAT THE FIRMWARE SAYS - the configured
; and measured rates of all four mailbox clocks - so the source can be
; NAMED rather than merely quantified. A measured 500 MHz means nothing
; until it is matched against the clock the firmware calls by that number.
;
; IT ALSO CHECKS SOMEBODY ELSE'S WORK. The FIRST row is the divider as
; found, measured before anything is touched and put back afterwards. So
; `i2c speed 400000`, then this, and the first row is the rate that
; command actually put on the wire - which is how the fix for this topic
; was proved rather than argued.
;
; BUILT WITH --entry-returns, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS SAFE TO RUN. It is sent
; to a resident Anvil and RETURNS to it, so a wrong answer costs a line of
; output rather than a power cycle:
;
;   pmfc RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4I2cScl.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o i2cscl.img
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN FIRST. `deadman 15` immediately before `run 400000`.
;
; WHAT IT LEAVES BEHIND. GPIO 2 and 3 on alt0, and the divider exactly as
; it was found. It writes no device: every transaction is addressed to
; 0x08, which the bus scan of 2026-09-03 found empty, and a read probe
; writes nothing even if something is there.

; THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING (a library never includes a
; library, so this file lists every one it needs, in a sequence that
; works). i2c.pi4 calls PinAlt out of gpio.pi4 and the two clock getters
; out of mailbox.pi4, so both must be parsed BEFORE it; timer.pi4 depends
; on nothing and can go anywhere.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/i2c.pi4"

#UART_DR = $FE201000
#UART_FR = $FE201018

; The address every transaction here is aimed at. 0x08 is the first
; address a scan tries and the 2026-09-03 pass found nothing at it. A
; read probe writes no byte, so even a device that appeared later would
; not be disturbed - only asked.
#SCL_PROBE_ADDR = $08

; How long one sampling window lasts, in microseconds. It only has to
; outlast one transaction at the slowest divider used here: nine SCL
; clocks at 65534 against a source as low as 150 MHz is about 3.9 ms, and
; against 500 MHz about 1.2 ms. 20 ms covers both with room to spare, and
; six windows still finish inside a fiftieth of the deadman.
#SCL_WINDOW_US = 20000

Procedure PutC(c.i)
  Define f.i
  Repeat
    f = PeekN(#UART_FR)
  Until (f & 32) = 0
  PokeL(#UART_DR, c)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutS(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekA(*s + i)
    If c = 0 : Break : EndIf
    PutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure PutNl()
  PutC(13) : PutC(10)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutDec(v.i)
  Define d.i
  Define started.i
  Define digit.i
  If v < 0
    PutC(45)
    v = 0 - v
  EndIf
  If v = 0
    PutC(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  d = 1000000000000000000
  started = 0
  While d > 0
    digit = (v / d) % 10
    If digit <> 0 Or started = 1
      PutC(48 + digit)
      started = 1
    EndIf
    d = d / 10
  Wend
EndProcedure

; A decimal number padded on the left to `w` columns, so the table lines
; up and a wrong order of magnitude is visible as a shape rather than
; having to be read digit by digit.
Procedure PutPad(v.i, w.i)
  Define n.i
  Define t.i
  n = 1
  t = v
  If t < 0
    t = 0 - t
    n = n + 1
  EndIf
  While t >= 10
    t = t / 10
    n = n + 1
  Wend
  While n < w
    PutC(32)
    n = n + 1
  Wend
  PutDec(v)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE MEASUREMENT ITSELF.
;
;  Results come back in globals rather than a struct because this
;  language has no multiple return and a diagnostic is a bad place to
;  invent one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global g_edges.i          ; falling edges seen on SCL during the window
Global g_samples.i        ; how many times the pad was actually read
Global g_windowticks.i    ; how long the window really was, in ticks
Global g_status.i         ; the BSC status word when the window closed
Global g_divback.i        ; DIV read back, so a rejected write is visible

; EVERY EDGE, NOT JUST THE FIRST AND LAST, AND HERE IS WHY.
;
; The first version of this instrument timed from the first falling edge
; to the last and divided by the number of gaps. It got a self-consistent
; answer that was five per cent low at every divider - which is exactly
; the shape of a real effect being averaged into a wrong model, and not
; the shape of noise. A transaction is not nine identical clock periods:
; it is eight address bits, an acknowledge slot, and then a STOP, and the
; controller does not have to spend one whole period on the last of those.
; Averaging over the tail folds that into the answer and biases it low, at
; every divider equally, so the six rows still AGREE with each other while
; all being wrong together. Agreement is not accuracy.
;
; So every edge is timestamped and the intervals are printed. The source
; is then computed only from the run of identical periods inside the
; address byte, and the odd interval at the end is visible in the table
; instead of hiding inside the number.
#SCL_MAX_EDGES = 24
Global Dim g_tick.i(#SCL_MAX_EDGES)

; The tick span the last row fitted over and how many clock periods that
; span was, so the two-divider slope below can subtract two of them. The
; count has to travel with the span: subtracting two spans measured over
; DIFFERENT numbers of periods is meaningless, and the caller checks.
Global g_fitspan.i
Global g_fitgaps.i

; ======================================================================
;  THE SAMPLING LOOP IS ASSEMBLY, AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE IN THIS TREE
;  WHERE THAT IS NOT A SHORTCUT.
;
;  The tree's rule is LIBRARIES, NEVER INTRINSICS, unless the language
;  genuinely cannot express the thing. Written in the language, this loop
;  samples at about 880 kHz - measured, printed by the instrument itself
;  as samples per SCL half-period. That is enough to resolve SCL down to
;  about 100 kHz and NOT enough for 400 kHz, which is exactly the rate
;  the topic asks to be proved.
;
;  WHY IT IS SO SLOW, AND WHY NO REWRITE IN THE LANGUAGE HELPS. The MMU
;  is off on this board, so there is NO DATA CACHE - and this compiler
;  gives every local variable a static home in memory. So each iteration
;  of a loop written normally costs one uncached DRAM access per variable
;  touched, plus the peripheral read it actually wanted. Eight or nine
;  DRAM round trips to look at one pin. It is not the compiler being
;  poor; it is the absence of a cache making "a variable" cost what a
;  memory access costs.
;
;  In registers the same loop is a peripheral load, a mask, two branches
;  and an add - and the peripheral load is then the only thing off-chip.
;  It measures about twenty times faster, which puts 400 kHz comfortably
;  inside reach.
;
;  THE INVARIANT, if this is ever edited: NOTHING IN THIS BLOCK MAY TOUCH
;  MEMORY except the peripheral read at [x0], the timestamp store at
;  [x14] on an edge, and the one sample-count store at the very end. A
;  single spilled variable in the middle of the loop and the sampling
;  rate goes back to where it was, silently, and the instrument starts
;  reporting a slower clock than the board has - which is precisely the
;  failure this whole topic is about.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i I2cSclSample(levaddr.i, mask.i, maxedges.i, *buf, deadline.i, *samples)
  ASM
    ; x0 = the GPLEV address          x3 = the timestamp buffer
    ; x1 = the SCL bit mask           x4 = the tick deadline
    ; x2 = how many the buffer holds  x5 = where the sample count goes
    ; x9  = this sample, masked       x13 = samples taken
    ; x10 = the previous sample       x14 = the buffer write pointer
    ; x11 = edges seen                x15 = the constant 255
    ; x12 = a timestamp, then scratch
    mov  x10, x1                 ; an idle bus rests HIGH - start there,
                                 ; so a line already low is not counted
                                 ; as a falling edge that never happened
    movz x11, #0
    movz x13, #0
    mov  x14, x3
    movz x15, #255
sclSampleLoop:
    ldr  w9, [x0]
    and  x9, x9, x1
    cbnz x9, sclSampleNext       ; high - cannot be a falling edge
    cbz  x10, sclSampleNext      ; was already low - not an edge either
    mrs  x12, cntpct_el0         ; a falling edge, and this is when
    cmp  x11, x2
    b.ge sclSampleCount          ; buffer full - still count it
    str  x12, [x14]
    add  x14, x14, #8
sclSampleCount:
    add  x11, x11, #1
sclSampleNext:
    mov  x10, x9
    add  x13, x13, #1
    ; The deadline is read once every 256 samples. Reading it every
    ; sample would add a system-register read to the hot path to police
    ; a window three orders of magnitude longer than the granularity
    ; that costs.
    and  x12, x13, x15
    cbnz x12, sclSampleLoop
    mrs  x12, cntpct_el0
    cmp  x12, x4
    b.lt sclSampleLoop
    str  x13, [x5]
    mov  x0, x11
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cSclWindow(cdiv.i, windowticks.i)
  Define tstart.i
  Define mask.i
  Define n.i

  mask = 1 << #I2C_PIN_SCL

  ; Program the divider and stage a one-byte read. Same register order
  ; the library's own I2cXfer uses (clear the sticky bits, address,
  ; length, then one write to C), because a different order here would
  ; be measuring a transaction shape the driver never produces.
  a64_barrier()
  PokeL(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_DIV_OFF, cdiv & $FFFF)
  g_divback = PeekN(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_DIV_OFF) & $FFFF
  PokeL(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_ERR | #BSC_S_CLKT | #BSC_S_DONE)
  PokeL(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_A_OFF, #SCL_PROBE_ADDR)
  PokeL(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_DLEN_OFF, 1)
  a64_barrier()

  g_edges = 0
  g_samples = 0

  tstart = Ticks()

  ; GO, and then straight into the sampler. Nothing may happen between
  ; the start bit and the loop: at the fast dividers one printed
  ; character would cost more than an SCL period.
  PokeL(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_C_OFF, #BSC_C_I2CEN | #BSC_C_CLEAR | #BSC_C_ST | #BSC_C_READ)
  n = I2cSclSample(#GPIO_BASE + #GPLEV0_OFF, mask, #SCL_MAX_EDGES, @g_tick(0), tstart + windowticks, @g_samples)

  g_windowticks = Ticks() - tstart
  a64_barrier()
  g_status = PeekN(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_S_OFF) & $FFFFFFFF
  PokeL(#BSC1_BASE + #BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_ERR | #BSC_S_CLKT | #BSC_S_DONE)
  a64_barrier()

  g_edges = n
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE FIRST GAP IS NOT A CLOCK PERIOD, AND IT WAS MEASURED THAT WAY.
;
;  The very first interval - from the first falling edge of SCL to the
;  second - comes out at almost exactly ONE AND A HALF times every
;  interval after it, at every divider:
;
;     cdiv 65534   10646  7067 7058 7097 7056 7086 7084 7074 7035
;     cdiv 32768    5277  3538 3539 3588 3537 3526 3523 3521 3580
;     cdiv 16384    2656  1779 1777 1790 1773 1772 1774 1730 1782
;
;  Every gap but the first is the period. The first is the START
;  condition's hold time plus the period, and the controller spends half
;  a period extra there.
;
;  THIS IS WHY THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS INSTRUMENT WAS WRONG BY EXACTLY
;  SEVEN PER CENT. It averaged over seven gaps including that one, so it
;  divided 7.5 periods' worth of ticks by 7 and reported 7/7.5 of the
;  real clock - 466 MHz where the answer is 500 MHz - at EVERY divider,
;  identically. Six rows agreeing with each other and all wrong together
;  is what a systematic error looks like, and it is why the intervals are
;  printed rather than summarised. The agreement was real. The number was
;  not.
;
;  So the fit starts at the SECOND edge and runs to the last one, over
;  gaps that are all the same thing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SCL_FIT_FROM = 1             ; skip gap 0 - the START hold, see above
#SCL_FIT_MIN_GAPS = 4         ; fewer than this is not a fit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  One row of the table, and the source clock it implies.
;  Returns the implied source in Hz, or 0 if the row is not evidence.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Row(cdiv.i, tickhz.i, windowticks.i)
  Define span.i
  Define gaps.i
  Define last.i
  Define scl.i
  Define src.i
  Define samplehz.i
  Define perhalf.i
  Define i.i
  Define g.i
  Define mean.i
  Define worst.i
  Define d.i

  I2cSclWindow(cdiv, windowticks)

  PutS("  cdiv ")
  PutPad(g_divback, 5)
  PutS("  edges ")
  PutPad(g_edges, 3)

  ; The fit runs from the second edge to the last one recorded. Every gap
  ; in that run is one clock period; the one before it is not.
  last = g_edges - 1
  If last > #SCL_MAX_EDGES - 1
    last = #SCL_MAX_EDGES - 1
  EndIf
  gaps = last - #SCL_FIT_FROM
  If gaps < #SCL_FIT_MIN_GAPS
    PutS("   TOO FEW EDGES to fit - ")
    PutDec(gaps)
    PutS(" usable gaps, wanted ")
    PutDec(#SCL_FIT_MIN_GAPS)
    PutS(", status $")
    PutDec(g_status)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  span = g_tick(last) - g_tick(#SCL_FIT_FROM)
  g_fitspan = span
  g_fitgaps = gaps
  If span <= 0
    PutS("   the first and last of the fitted edges carry the same")
    PutS(" timestamp - nothing can be divided by that.")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; SCL and then the source, each in ONE expression rather than from the
  ; rounded value above it, so a printed rounding does not propagate.
  ; gaps * tickhz * cdiv is at most about 3e13 here, a long way inside a
  ; 64-bit product.
  scl = (gaps * tickhz) / span
  src = (gaps * tickhz * cdiv) / span

  samplehz = (g_samples * tickhz) / g_windowticks
  perhalf = samplehz / (scl * 2)

  ; HOW UNIFORM THE FITTED GAPS ACTUALLY WERE, as the worst single
  ; deviation from their mean, in per cent. This is the number that says
  ; whether the row is one clock measured a few times or a sampling loop
  ; losing edges, and it costs one pass over an array that is already in
  ; registers. A row with a large scatter is still usable - the mean is
  ; what is quoted - but the reader gets to see the spread rather than a
  ; confident single figure resting on it.
  mean = span / gaps
  worst = 0
  For i = #SCL_FIT_FROM + 1 To last
    g = g_tick(i) - g_tick(i - 1)
    d = g - mean
    If d < 0
      d = 0 - d
    EndIf
    If d > worst
      worst = d
    EndIf
  Next

  PutS("  SCL ")
  PutPad(scl, 8)
  PutS(" Hz   source ")
  PutPad(src, 10)
  PutS(" Hz   samp/half ")
  PutPad(perhalf, 4)
  PutS("  spread ")
  PutPad((worst * 100) / mean, 3)
  PutC(37)                            ; 37 = '%'

  ; EVERY INTERVAL, IN TICKS, so the reader can see for themselves that
  ; the fitted ones are uniform and that the first one is not. A number
  ; that has to be trusted is worth less than a number with its working
  ; printed beside it - and this is the working that caught the seven per
  ; cent the header describes.
  PutS("   gaps")
  For i = 1 To last
    If i = #SCL_FIT_FROM + 1
      PutS("  |")                     ; everything right of the bar is fitted
    EndIf
    PutC(32)
    PutDec(g_tick(i) - g_tick(i - 1))
  Next
  If perhalf < 4
    PutS("   <- TOO COARSE, not evidence")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  ProcedureReturn src
EndProcedure

Procedure SayClock(*name, id.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS("  configured ")
  PutPad(MailboxClockRate(id), 11)
  PutS("   measured ")
  PutPad(MailboxClockRateMeasured(id), 11)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SLOPE, AND WHY IT IS A BETTER NUMBER THAN ANY SINGLE ROW.
;
;  Every row above divides a measured tick span by a known number of
;  periods, and it carries one systematic error nobody can remove from a
;  single reading: a polling loop notices an edge some time AFTER it
;  happens, and that latency sits inside the span. It is roughly the same
;  at both ends of a span, so it mostly cancels - but "mostly" is not a
;  measurement.
;
;  Two dividers remove it properly. The fitted span is the same known
;  number of periods at both, so
;
;     span(cdiv) = gaps * cdiv / source + latency
;
;  and the latency, being a property of the sampling loop and not of the
;  divider, subtracts out exactly:
;
;     source = gaps * (cdivA - cdivB) * tick_hz / (spanA - spanB)
;
;  Nothing here is assumed about the transaction, the controller or the
;  loop. Two spans, two dividers, one known period count.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SlopeEstimate(spanA.i, cdivA.i, spanB.i, cdivB.i, gaps.i, tickhz.i)
  If spanA <= spanB Or cdivA <= cdivB Or gaps <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (gaps * (cdivA - cdivB) * tickhz) / (spanA - spanB)
EndProcedure

Procedure Main()
  Define tickhz.i
  Define windowticks.i
  Define s1.i
  Define s2.i
  Define s3.i
  Define s4.i
  Define s5.i
  Define good.i
  Define total.i
  Define avg.i
  Define slope.i
  Define spanslow.i
  Define spanfast.i
  Define gapsslow.i
  Define gapsfast.i
  Define core.i
  Define asfound.i

  PutNl()
  PutS("=== Pi 4 I2C source clock - measured on the wire, not read off a page ===")
  PutNl()

  tickhz = TickHz()
  If tickhz <= 0
    PutS("CNTFRQ_EL0 reads ")
    PutDec(tickhz)
    PutS(" - nothing here can be timed. Stopping.")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  PutS("tick counter ")
  PutDec(tickhz)
  PutS(" Hz")
  PutNl()

  If MailboxInit() = 0
    PutS("MailboxInit failed, error ")
    PutDec(MailboxError())
    PutS(" - the firmware clocks cannot be named, but the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("measurement below does not need them.")
    PutNl()
  Else
    PutS("what the firmware says about its clocks:")
    PutNl()
    SayClock("ARM ", #MBX_CLK_ARM)
    SayClock("CORE", #MBX_CLK_CORE)
    SayClock("EMMC", #MBX_CLK_EMMC)
    SayClock("UART", #MBX_CLK_UART)
  EndIf

  ; THE DIVIDER AS FOUND, READ BEFORE ANYTHING IS TOUCHED. This is what
  ; makes the instrument able to check somebody ELSE's work: type
  ; `i2c speed 400000`, run this, and the first row below is the rate
  ; that command actually put on the wire, measured. It is put back at
  ; the end, so running this does not disturb the setting it came to
  ; check.
  asfound = I2cGetDiv()

  ; Bring the bus up only if it is not already up. I2cInit REPROGRAMS
  ; THE DIVIDER, so calling it unconditionally would destroy the very
  ; setting the row above exists to measure. If the pins are already on
  ; the controller's function there is nothing to bring up.
  ;
  ; Until the bus has been up once, the SCL pad is a plain input
  ; connected to nothing the controller drives - the unmuxed-pins defect, and
  ; it would make every row below read zero edges.
  If I2cPinsMuxed() <> 1
    PutS("the bus was not up, so it is being brought up here - which resets the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("divider, so the as-found row is the 100 kHz default and not a setting.")
    PutNl()
    I2cInit()
    asfound = I2cGetDiv()
  EndIf
  If I2cPinsMuxed() <> 1
    PutS("the bus pins are NOT on the controller's function after I2cInit,")
    PutNl()
    PutS("so nothing below would be measuring SCL. Stopping.")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 2
  EndIf

  windowticks = (tickhz / 1000) * (#SCL_WINDOW_US / 1000)

  PutNl()
  PutS("AS FOUND - the divider whoever ran last left behind, measured:")
  PutNl()
  Row(asfound, tickhz, windowticks)

  PutNl()
  PutS("measuring SCL directly, one window per divider:")
  PutNl()
  s1 = Row(65534, tickhz, windowticks)
  spanslow = g_fitspan                    ; kept for the two-divider slope
  gapsslow = g_fitgaps
  s2 = Row(32768, tickhz, windowticks)
  s3 = Row(16384, tickhz, windowticks)
  s4 = Row(8192, tickhz, windowticks)
  spanfast = g_fitspan                    ; the other end of the slope
  gapsfast = g_fitgaps
  s5 = Row(4096, tickhz, windowticks)
  Row(1500, tickhz, windowticks)          ; the reset default, for the record

  good = 0
  total = 0
  If s1 > 0 : good = good + 1 : total = total + s1 : EndIf
  If s2 > 0 : good = good + 1 : total = total + s2 : EndIf
  If s3 > 0 : good = good + 1 : total = total + s3 : EndIf
  If s4 > 0 : good = good + 1 : total = total + s4 : EndIf
  If s5 > 0 : good = good + 1 : total = total + s5 : EndIf

  PutNl()
  If good = 0
    PutS("NO ROW IS EVIDENCE. Nothing was measured; do not quote a number.")
    PutNl()
    I2cSetDiv(asfound)
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf

  avg = total / good
  PutS("mean of the ")
  PutDec(good)
  PutS(" usable rows: source = ")
  PutDec(avg)
  PutS(" Hz")
  PutNl()

  ; THE TWO-DIVIDER SLOPE, which removes the sampling latency the mean
  ; above still carries. cdiv 65534 against cdiv 8192, the slowest row
  ; and the fastest one still sampled comfortably.
  slope = 0
  If gapsslow = gapsfast
    slope = SlopeEstimate(spanslow, 65534, spanfast, 8192, gapsslow, tickhz)
  Else
    PutS("no slope: the two rows fitted ")
    PutDec(gapsslow)
    PutS(" and ")
    PutDec(gapsfast)
    PutS(" periods, and spans over")
    PutNl()
    PutS("different period counts cannot be subtracted.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  If slope > 0
    PutS("two-divider slope, sampling latency subtracted out: source = ")
    PutDec(slope)
    PutS(" Hz")
    PutNl()
  EndIf

  ; AND NOW NAME IT. A measured half-gigahertz means nothing until it is
  ; matched against a clock the firmware will talk about by name; that is
  ; what turns "the divider is fed by something near 500 MHz" into "the
  ; divider is fed by the clock the mailbox calls CORE, and here is the
  ; ratio". Printed as a per-mille error so a one-to-one match is not
  ; something the reader has to work out.
  core = MailboxClockRateMeasured(#MBX_CLK_CORE)
  If core > 0 And slope > 0
    PutS("the firmware's MEASURED core clock is ")
    PutDec(core)
    PutS(" Hz; the slope is ")
    PutDec((slope * 1000) / core)
    PutS("/1000 of it")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  core = MailboxClockRate(#MBX_CLK_CORE)
  If core > 0 And slope > 0
    PutS("its CONFIGURED core clock is ")
    PutDec(core)
    PutS(" Hz; the slope is ")
    PutDec((slope * 1000) / core)
    PutS("/1000 of that")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  If slope > 0
    PutS("the datasheet's nominal 150000000 Hz; the slope is ")
    PutDec((slope * 1000) / 150000000)
    PutS("/1000 of that")
    PutNl()
  EndIf

  ; PUT THE DIVIDER BACK EXACTLY AS IT WAS FOUND. An instrument that
  ; changes the thing it came to measure is worth nothing on the second
  ; run, and `i2c speed` typed after this must report what it reported
  ; before it.
  I2cSetDiv(asfound)

  PutS("=== done ===")
  PutNl()
  ProcedureReturn avg / 1000000
EndProcedure
